[FINAL-TEXT] **Root cause found.** `infra_sweep` flagged `router6` with iptables FORWARD rules that **drop all traffic arriving on eth2**:

```
-A FORWARD -i eth2 -p tcp -j DROP
-A FORWARD -i eth2 -p udp -j DROP
-A FORWARD -i eth2 -j DROP
```

From the topology: `router6.eth2 = r6_r3` — the only uplink connecting router6 to router3 (and through it, to the entire rest of the network). Any traffic arriving from router3 on that interface gets silently dropped before it can be forwarded toward mswitch2 and the 172.18.51.x hosts. The reverse path (172.18.51.x hosts originating traffic to other subnets) also fails because ICMP/TCP/UDP replies must return via router3→eth2→router6, where they hit this DROP wall.

This perfectly explains the symptom pattern: intra-site (172.18.51.x) works (it never touches eth2), but all inter-subnet communication fails in both directions. The pc4→pc14 path stays healthy because it routes through mswitch1→router0, never touching router6.

This is a confirmed ACL/firewall fault. No further probes needed — direct iptables evidence on the owning device matches the fingerprint exactly.

