=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** The iptables FORWARD chain on router6 contains three rules that unconditionally DROP all packets arriving on interface eth2 — the physical uplink to router3 (`r6_r3` link):
  - `-A FORWARD -i eth2 -p tcp -j DROP`
  - `-A FORWARD -i eth2 -p udp -j DROP`
  - `-A FORWARD -i eth2 -j DROP` (catch-all, including ICMP)

**Mechanism:** router6 is the sole L3 gateway connecting the 172.18.51.0/x site (pc15–pc18 behind switch10 and mswitch2) to the rest of the network. Its only WAN uplink is eth2 (to router3). Any traffic directed toward the 172.18.51.x site from any other subnet enters router6 on eth2 and is immediately silently dropped by the FORWARD chain. Similarly, traffic initiated by 172.18.51.x hosts that reaches a remote subnet generates ICMP/UDP/TCP replies that must return via router3→eth2, where they are also dropped — making every session appear to time out bidirectionally.

**Localization:** router6, interface eth2 (`r6_r3` collision domain). The drop rules are in the kernel iptables FORWARD chain. No dynamic routing daemon (OSPF/BGP) is running anywhere in this topology — all routing is static — so there is no protocol mechanism to reroute around this.

**Root cause:** Three iptables FORWARD DROP rules on router6 block all forwarded traffic arriving on eth2, severing the entire 172.18.51.x site from the rest of the network.

**Fix:** Remove the offending rules on router6: `iptables -D FORWARD -i eth2 -p tcp -j DROP; iptables -D FORWARD -i eth2 -p udp -j DROP; iptables -D FORWARD -i eth2 -j DROP` and ensure no equivalent rules are persisted in `/etc/iptables/rules.v4` or similar.
=== END ===